Schema drift happens when:<p><pre><code>    User documents start with { name: &quot;John&quot;, email: &quot;john@...&quot; }

    Later, someone adds { name: &quot;Jane&quot;, email: &quot;jane@...&quot;, profile: {...} }

    Even later: { name: &quot;Bob&quot;, email: &quot;bob@...&quot;, profile: &quot;basic&quot; }
</code></pre>
Now profile is sometimes an object, sometimes a string, sometimes missing entirely.<p>When this breaks:<p>javascript&#x2F;&#x2F; This works for some docs, fails for others
user.profile.avatar &#x2F;&#x2F; TypeError: Cannot read property &#x27;avatar&#x27; of undefined<p>Security gaps emerge because:<p><pre><code>    You write rules assuming a consistent schema: allow read: if resource.data.profile.role == &quot;admin&quot;

    But when profile is a string or missing, this rule behaves unexpectedly (usually throwing evaluation errors and blocking access for legitimate users, or worse, leaving loopholes if rules are overly permissive).

